## Why This Is Asked
Tests practical knowledge of transactional memory in real workloads, careful fallback design, and low-latency queues.

## Key Concepts
- Intel TSX (_xbegin/_xend)
- CAS-based fallback paths
- Per-slot sequence counters to avoid ABA
- Cache-line alignment and false sharing avoidance
- Microbenchmarking for latency and throughput

## Code Example
```c
// Skeleton TSX-based enqueue
typedef struct { unsigned long long seq; unsigned long long val; } slot_t;
typedef struct { slot_t *slots; unsigned head, tail, cap; } q_t;
static inline int enqueue_htm(q_t *q, unsigned long long val) {
    unsigned pos = q->tail;
    if ((pos + 1) % q->cap == q->head) return -1; // full

    unsigned status = _xbegin();
    if (status == _XBEGIN_STARTED) {
        q->slots[pos].val = val;
        q->slots[pos].seq = pos + 1;
        q->tail = (pos + 1) % q->cap;
        _xend();
        return 0;
    }

    // Fallback: CAS-based enqueue with sequence validation
    return enqueue_cas(q, val);
}
```

## Design Considerations
- Retry limits: 3-5 TSX attempts before permanent fallback
- Alignment: 64-byte cache line boundaries
- Contention handling: exponential backoff in fallback path
- Benchmark: measure 99th percentile latency under 1 vs 16 producer/consumer pairs

## Why This Question Is Asked

This question evaluates the ability to design crash-safe, lock-free data structures for non-volatile memory systems. It tests understanding of durability guarantees, memory ordering semantics, and recovery mechanisms.

## Key Concepts

- **Durable memory semantics** in NVRAM environments
- **Log-structured queue design** for write optimization
- **Per-producer segmentation** to reduce contention
- **Atomic tail descriptors** and sequence counters to mitigate ABA problems
- **Recovery replay** mechanisms and correctness guarantees

## Code Example

```c
typedef struct {
    volatile uint64_t head;
    volatile uint64_t tail;
    _Atomic uint64_t durable_tail;
    void *segments[NUM_SEGMENTS];
} NVRAMQueue;

// Pseudo-code for enqueue
int enqueue(NVRAMQueue *q, void *data) {
    uint64_t segment = get_segment_id();
    uint64_t idx = atomic_fetch_add(&q->tail, 1) % SEGMENT_SIZE;

    // Write data and flush to NVRAM
    write_and_flush(&q->segments[segment][idx], data);

    // Memory barrier before advancing durable tail
    atomic_thread_fence(memory_order_release);

    // Atomically advance durable tail
    uint64_t old = atomic_load(&q->durable_tail);
    atomic_compare_exchange_strong(&q->durable_tail, &old, old + 1);
    return 0;
}

// Pseudo-code for dequeue with recovery safety
void *dequeue(NVRAMQueue *q) {
    uint64_t durable = atomic_load(&q->durable_tail);
    uint64_t current = atomic_load(&q->head);

    if (current >= durable) return NULL; // Nothing to dequeue

    // Read with acquire barrier
    atomic_thread_fence(memory_order_acquire);

    return read_and_invalidate(&q->segments[current % SEGMENT_SIZE]);
}

// Recovery procedure
int recover(NVRAMQueue *q) {
    uint64_t durable = atomic_load(&q->durable_tail);
    uint64_t head = atomic_load(&q->head);

    // Replay log to find last consistent state
    while (head < durable) {
        validate_and_replay_entry(q, head);
        head++;
    }

    atomic_store(&q->head, durable);
    return 0;
}
```

## Discussion Points

### Memory Ordering Barriers
- Use `memory_order_release` before advancing the durable tail to ensure all writes are visible
- Use `memory_order_acquire` when reading the tail to synchronize with previous producers

### Cache-Line Alignment
- Align each segment to cache-line boundaries (typically 64 bytes) to prevent false sharing
- Pad metadata structures to avoid contention between producers

### Failure Safety
- The durable tail only advances after NVRAM flushes complete
- Recovery replays from the last known head position to the durable tail
- Checkpoint periodically to bound recovery time

### Microbenchmark Plan
1. **Mixed contention**: Run multiple producers/consumers with varying workloads
2. **Simulated power loss**: Use fsync/fence operations to model durability
3. **Metrics**: Measure throughput, latency, and recovery time after crash
4. **Test scenarios**: Single producer, multiple producers, high contention, and crash-recovery sequences

## How It Works

A 2MB arena is divided into 64-byte blocks, yielding 32768 blocks. The free list is a single atomic head packed into a 64-bit value: [ version (upper 32 bits) | head index (lower 32 bits) ]. A free block stores the index of the next free block. On init, all blocks form a chain starting at 0, ending with a sentinel (0xFFFFFFFF).

The allocator uses a lock-free approach:
- Alloc: read the head with acquire, compute the next pointer, CAS to update head to next and increment version. Return the block corresponding to the popped index.
- Free: write the freed block’s next pointer to current head, CAS to push it back and increment version.

ABA is mitigated by the version counter; each successful CAS increments the version, so a stale head cannot be mistaken for a newer one.

Memory order choices:
- Loads use memory_order_acquire to synchronize with the producer/consumer.
- Stores use memory_order_release or memory_order_acq_rel to ensure visibility of the updated head and block linkage.

### Key Points
- **64-bit head encoding**: version guards against ABA by differentiating sequential head values.
- **Block linkage stored in arena**: next indices reside in the block headers themselves.
- **Initialization**: pre-link 32768 blocks and set head to 0 with version 1.
- **Threading model**: works with many producers/consumers with minimal contention due to CAS on a single head.

### Example
```c
#include <stdatomic.h>
#include <stdint.h>
#include <stddef.h>

#define ARENA_SIZE (2*1024*1024)
#define BLOCK_SIZE 64
#define BLOCKS (ARENA_SIZE / BLOCK_SIZE)

typedef struct { uint32_t next; } Block;
static uint8_t arena[ARENA_SIZE];
static _Atomic uint64_t head; // [version:upper32 | index:lower32]

static inline void init_slab(void) {
  for (uint32_t i = 0; i < BLOCKS-1; ++i) ((Block*)arena)[i].next = i+1;
  ((Block*)arena)[BLOCKS-1].next = 0xFFFFFFFFu;
  atomic_store_explicit(&head, ((uint64_t)1 << 32) | 0u, memory_order_relaxed);
}

void* slab_alloc(void) {
  uint64_t oldv, newv;
  uint32_t idx, ver, next;
  while (1) {
    oldv = atomic_load_explicit(&head, memory_order_acquire);
    idx = (uint32_t)(oldv & 0xFFFFFFFFu);
    ver = (uint32_t)(oldv >> 32);
    if (idx == 0xFFFFFFFFu) return NULL;
    next = ((Block*)arena)[idx].next;
    newv = ((uint64_t)(ver+1) << 32) | (uint64_t)next;
    if (atomic_compare_exchange_weak_explicit(&head, &oldv, newv,
                                            memory_order_acquire, memory_order_relaxed))
      return (void*)(arena + (size_t)idx * BLOCK_SIZE);
  }
}

void slab_free(void* p) {
  if (!p) return;
  uint32_t idx = ((uint8_t*)p - arena) / BLOCK_SIZE;
  uint64_t oldv, newv;
  while (1) {
    oldv = atomic_load_explicit(&head, memory_order_acquire);
    uint32_t ver = (uint32_t)(oldv >> 32);
    ((Block*)arena)[idx].next = (uint32_t)(oldv & 0xFFFFFFFFu);
    newv = ((uint64_t)(ver+1) << 32) | idx;
    if (atomic_compare_exchange_weak_explicit(&head, &oldv, newv,
                                            memory_order_release, memory_order_relaxed))
      break;
  }
}

/* Simple multithreaded test omitted for brevity; see test harness in real usage */
```

